module dev.slatelang.slate

import sh.sysl.gc
import sh.sysl.parsing.*
import sysl.buf.{Buf, buf}
import sysl.container.{Map, map}

// The collected heap, and the objects in it.
//
// **Reference counting cannot free a cycle, and slate makes one on every named function.** `define`
// binds a closure into the very scope that closure captured, so the scope holds the closure and the
// closure holds the scope. Nothing declared that back-edge, so `weak` cannot help: it is a cycle in
// somebody else's program, which is the case `sh.sysl.gc` exists for.
//
// ## What is collected and what is not
//
// **The collector manages exactly what refcounting cannot.** A string, an array, an object, a
// closure and a scope are collected, because a value graph made of them can cycle. The *tree* is
// not: an `Expr` is immutable and acyclic, so ARC frees it correctly and it stays where it is.
//
// A slate string is a GC object holding a sysl `string`, which its finalizer releases -- so a string
// takes part in the collector's lifetime the way Go's does, without this file reimplementing every
// string operation over raw bytes. The same bargain gives an array its `Buf` and an object its
// `Map`.
//
// **`Value` therefore holds no reference-counted member at all**: scalars and raw pointers, nothing
// else. That is what lets one be stored into GC memory, copied into a `Buf`, and passed around with
// no ARC traffic on the interpreter's hot path.

// How many bytes the collected heap gets by default.
//
// One block, fixed when the VM is made -- there is no growing, and `alloc` answers null when it is
// full. **A VM asks for its own ceiling** (`new_vm(ceiling)` in `vm_state.sysl`, which `malloc`s the
// block), and this is what the program's own VM asks for, so the default behaviour is what it was.
//
// **Four megabytes was the first number and it was too small to build anything with**: about twelve
// thousand objects with a proto, fewer than a program reading a modest CSV would make. This holds
// something over four hundred thousand.
//
// **THE SIZE USED TO COST BUILD TIME, AND THAT WAS A SYSL DEFECT RATHER THAN A FACT ABOUT SLATE.**
// Compiling this file once took time proportional to the size of the array -- 12 s at 4 MiB, 116 s at
// 64, and 256 MiB never finished -- though the array is zero-initialised and the linker emits it as
// BSS, so the binary never grew. `StaticEmitter.constantValue` lowered `[0; N]` as `List.fill(N)` of
// the element constant, handing LLVM an N-element array literal where `zeroinitializer` says the same
// thing in one word.
//
// **Fixed in sysl 0.0.86, card 0319, and confirmed from this end.** The IR is now flat: 5,129,651
// bytes at 4 MiB against 5,129,657 at 256 MiB, a difference of six -- the digits in the number. The
// global reads `[268435456 x i8] zeroinitializer` and the whole build takes ten seconds.
//
// **It reproduced only on MODULE storage.** The same declaration inside a function is a body local and
// lowers to an `alloca` and a fill loop, flat at every size -- sysl's first reduction was a single
// entry file and showed nothing, which nearly sent the diagnosis the other way. The arena was a
// module-level `[HeapBytes]u8` for exactly that reason and is a `malloc` now, so none of this binds
// any more; it is kept because the sysl defect is worth knowing about before somebody writes the
// next big array.
//
// **A `malloc`ed block is NOT zero-filled and nothing here needs it to be**: `gc.alloc` zeroes every
// payload before the caller sees it, which its own doc says is what makes storing a reference-counted
// member safe. **`malloc` rather than `calloc` is the whole of what keeps the old BSS bargain** --
// the pages cost address space and are committed by the operating system only as they are touched,
// so a program that uses a megabyte still pays for a megabyte rather than for 256 of them.
// Not `private`: `new_vm` in `vm_state.sysl` gives the program's own VM this ceiling.
const HeapBytes: usize = 268435456

// The heap this line of execution allocates in.
//
// **A `*gc.Heap` accessor rather than a global, which is the `machine()`/`running_unit()` shape**, so
// every site below reads `slate_heap()` where it read the global, and nothing else moved. The block
// under it is `current().region`, `malloc`ed by `new_vm` and given back by `dispose_vm`.
slate_heap() -> *gc.Heap = &current().heap

// How much of that block a program may actually have.
//
// **`current().heap_ceiling` is the block and `current().heap_limit` is the setting**, which lets a test
// about running out of memory run out of a small one. Filling 256 MiB to find out what happens at the
// end of it takes nearly five seconds; filling one takes no time at all, and the behaviour under test
// is the same either way. Nothing but the suite changes it, and `run` puts it back.

// Give the next program a heap of this size. `reset_heap` is what acts on it.
// **The ceiling is this VM's own block and not `HeapBytes`**, which is the whole of what a per-VM
// region changed here: a VM spawned with a megabyte cannot be asked for more than a megabyte, where
// the shared arena would have handed it 256.
// **A named limit below the block bounds PAYLOAD too**, which is what `Vm.payload_capped` says and
// is the whole of how a test measures that bound: the program's own VM is uncapped by default, and
// putting the limit back to the block puts that default back with it.
set_heap_limit(bytes: usize)
    val v = current()

    v.heap_limit = if bytes > v.heap_ceiling then v.heap_ceiling else bytes
    v.payload_capped = v.heap_limit < v.heap_ceiling

// -- what a cell owns outside the heap ------------------------------------------------------------
//
// **A cell is small and what it owns is not**, so a heap measured in cells alone says almost nothing
// about what a program is holding: a one-megabyte byte buffer is a `BytesObj` of a few words with a
// megabyte of counted storage hanging off it. Neither the collection schedule nor the ceiling could
// see that storage, which made two separate failures out of one hole -- garbage piling up because a
// collection was never due, and an actor outgrowing the heap it named without ever being told.
//
// **`current().payload` is the figure and it is kept two ways at once.** Every site that allocates
// or grows a payload charges it, and every site that hands one back credits it; and because a charge
// without its credit would grow for ever, the mark phase recomputes the whole figure from the live
// objects at every collection. So a missed charge costs a stale schedule until the next collection
// and can never become a false out-of-memory.
//
// **The measure is CAPACITY and not length.** A `Buf` that has grown to a thousand slots is holding
// a thousand slots' worth of storage whatever its count says, and `clear` and `pop` give none of it
// back -- so charging by length would report a buffer a program emptied as costing nothing while the
// pages are still committed.

// Take note of payload just allocated.
charge(n: usize)
    current().payload += n

// Take note of payload just given back. **Clamped at zero**: the figure is an estimate between
// collections, and a credit larger than the running count must not wrap a `usize`.
credit(n: usize)
    val v = current()

    v.payload = if v.payload > n then v.payload - n else 0

// Move the count from one footprint to another, which is what a container that was regrown owes.
recharge(was: usize, now: usize)
    if now > was
        charge(now - was)
    elif was > now
        credit(was - now)

// What the heap and everything hanging off it hold, which is the number both the schedule and the
// ceiling read. **Cells alone is the figure that took a bug to learn was wrong.**
heap_in_use() -> usize = gc.live_size(slate_heap()) + current().payload

// -- the objects --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A slate string. Immutable, as Go's is, so nothing ever writes through one.
//
// **The GC object is the sysl string's ONLY owner, and that is the point of the arrangement.**
// `new_str` takes the string by value and stores it; the caller's copy dies with the caller's frame,
// which drops the count to one -- the one inside this object. From then on nothing but the
// collector decides when those bytes go, and `finalize_str` is what takes the count to zero. A
// caller that squirrels a second copy away somewhere would break exactly that, which is why nothing
// in slate holds a sysl `string` for a slate string value: a `Value` holds a `*StrObj` and nothing
// else.
// **THE CELL CARRIES THE CHARACTER COUNT, AND IT IS NEVER WORKED OUT TWICE.** A slate position is a
// character and the bytes are UTF-8, so `s.length` and `s[i]` used to walk the string every time
// they were asked -- which makes an ordinary left-to-right read quadratic. The count is written when
// the cell is made and a string is immutable, so it can never go stale.
//
// **`chars == s.bytes.len` IS THE TEST FOR "EVERY CHARACTER IS ONE BYTE"**, and that falls out of
// keeping the count rather than costing a flag of its own. For such a string a character position
// *is* a byte offset, so indexing and slicing are arithmetic.
//
// **The cursor is the answer for a string that is NOT all one-byte**: the last position asked about
// and the byte offset it landed on, so a walk forwards or backwards costs the distance from the last
// question rather than the distance from the start. `(0, 0)` is what a zeroed cell holds and is also
// the right answer for character zero, so there is no sentinel and nothing to initialise.
//
// **It is mutable state on an immutable value and it needs no lock.** A string never crosses a
// thread by reference -- an actor message copies every value into the receiving heap -- so the only
// writer of a cell's cursor is the one VM that can reach it.
struct StrObj
    s: string
    chars: usize
    cursor_char: usize
    cursor_byte: usize

// A slate array. Its elements are `Value`s, which is what `trace` walks.
struct ArrayObj
    items: Buf[Value]

// A slate byte buffer: a run of raw bytes, one byte per byte, that a program may grow and write
// through.
//
// **A `Buf[u8]` and not a `Buf[Value]`, and that is the whole reason this kind exists.** An array of
// numbers holding bytes costs a `Value` per byte -- sixteen bytes of storage for one byte of data --
// and every read of one goes through a tagged union. Here a byte is a byte, `length` is `data.len()`,
// and appending a whole buffer to another is one `extend` rather than a loop in the interpreter.
//
// **Nothing in the region is a `Value`, so the tracer walks nothing** -- `trace_bytes` is empty for
// `trace_str`'s reason, and the `Buf` is reference counted outside the collected heap the way a
// string's `string` and an array's `Buf` are, given back by the finalizer.
struct BytesObj
    data: Buf[u8]

// A slate object: a record, keyed by any value at all.
//
// **The layout is `table.sysl`'s compact dict**, so the fields keep the order they were written in
// and the tracer below is a linear walk rather than a walk of somebody else's container.
struct ObjectObj
    entries: Buf[Entry]
    index: Buf[usize]

    // How many entries are not tombstones.
    live: usize

    // Whether this object is a DATA VALUE, which does not change.
    //
    // **A field rather than a question asked of the proto chain**, which is what the alternative
    // would have been: a walk on every field write, paid by every object, to answer something that
    // is settled the moment the object is built.
    frozen: bool

    // Whether this table finds a key by IDENTITY rather than by what it holds.
    //
    // **A weak map and nothing else**, and it is a field for `frozen`'s reason: it is decided where
    // the table is made and never changes, so a walk asking it per lookup would be paying for an
    // answer that was settled at the allocation. `table.sysl`'s `key_hash` and `keys_match` are the
    // two places it is read.
    by_identity: bool

// A function and the scope it was written in.
//
// **The scope is the whole of what makes it a closure**, and the reason this file exists: a function
// that outlives the call that made it keeps that scope alive, and a named function's scope holds the
// function.
struct ClosureObj
    params: Buf[string]

    // An index into `bodies`, not an `Expr`: a counted box may not live in raw GC memory.
    body: usize

    scope: *EnvObj

// A value that is not there yet.
//
// **It is a collected object like any other, and it has to be**: a promise outlives the call that
// made it by definition, is held by whoever awaits it *and* by the coroutine that will settle it, and
// those two release it in an order nobody controls.
//
// **What it holds that an object does not is a list of lines of execution.** A machine waiting here
// is parked, and its id is what this keeps -- an index into `parked` rather than a pointer, so
// nothing in the collected heap has to know what a `Machine` is.
struct PromiseObj
    // What it settled to. Meaningless until `settled`, and `Null` when it failed.
    value: Value

    settled: bool
    failed: bool

    // The complaint and where it was raised, for a promise that failed. A `string` lives here the way
    // `StrObj`'s does, with the finalizer below giving it back.
    message: string
    at: Span

    // **Which file `at` is an offset into, carried the way a `Fail` carries its own `src`.** A
    // promise outlives the call that settled it, so the file has to travel with the span rather
    // than being read off whatever the machine happens to be running when the rejection is finally
    // reported -- that mismatch is what sent a fault raised in one file to a byte offset in another.
    src: usize

    // **Whether this promise failed because `exit` was called, and the status it was given.** A
    // rejection that crosses an `await` is otherwise flattened to a message and a span, which loses
    // whether it was `Quit` or an ordinary `Fail` -- and `Quit` has to be told apart from every other
    // failure everywhere it resurfaces, because it is not a diagnostic anywhere but `slate test`; it
    // is the program's own exit.
    quit: bool
    quit_status: long

    // The parked machines to hand the answer to. Emptied as they are queued.
    waiters: Buf[usize]

    // Whether anything has ever awaited this.
    //
    // **This is what tells a rejection that was dealt with from one that vanished.** A promise that
    // fails with nothing waiting on it is the commonest way an async program loses an error -- node
    // calls it an unhandled rejection and warns about it, and slate reports it as the program's fault.
    handled: bool

    // Whether the program threw this promise away -- a call whose value was discarded, which is what
    // `main()` on a line of its own is.
    //
    // **This is what says a rejection can be reported NOW rather than at the end of the run.** The
    // question "was this handled?" normally cannot be answered when a promise fails, because `val p =
    // risky()` and `await p` three lines later is ordinary -- so the answer waits until everything has
    // settled. A promise nobody was given is the one case where it can be answered at once: there is
    // no name for it and no line that could await it, so the failure is final the moment it happens.
    //
    // Without this a fault inside a coroutine is silent for as long as the program lives, which for a
    // server is forever -- the diagnostic sits in `rejections` waiting for a drain that never comes,
    // and what the program looks like from outside is a hang.
    orphaned: bool

// A hold on a value that does not keep it alive.
//
// **One slot and nothing else**, so there is no finalizer: a `Value` carries no reference-counted
// member, which is what lets a zeroed payload be a valid one of these from the moment `alloc`
// answers -- a zeroed `Value` reads as `Null`, and `Null` is exactly what a cleared reference holds.
struct WeakRefObj
    target: Value

// A scope: the names written in one block, and the scope it sits inside.
struct EnvObj
    names: Map[string, Value]

    // Which names may be written to. A `val` is absent and a `var` is present.
    mutable: Map[string, bool]

    // Null at the top level, where there is nothing outside.
    parent: *EnvObj

// -- tracing ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Grey whatever a value points at. Scalars point at nothing and cost one comparison.
mark_value(h: *gc.Heap, v: Value)
    v match
        Str(p) -> gc.mark(h, ptr_cast(p))
        Array(p) -> gc.mark(h, ptr_cast(p))
        Bytes(p) -> gc.mark(h, ptr_cast(p))
        Object(p) -> gc.mark(h, ptr_cast(p))

        // **The same object kind, so the same tracer walks it**: a set and a map are an
        // `ObjectObj`, and `trace_object` already marks every key and every value in one.
        SetVal(p) -> gc.mark(h, ptr_cast(p))
        MapVal(p) -> gc.mark(h, ptr_cast(p))
        Fn(p) -> gc.mark(h, ptr_cast(p))
        Promise(p) -> gc.mark(h, ptr_cast(p))

        // **The weak pair is marked STRONGLY from outside and weakly from inside**, which is not a
        // contradiction: a weak map is an ordinary object as far as everything holding *it* is
        // concerned, and it is the entries inside it that do not keep their keys. A value naming one
        // has to mark it, or a weak map held in a variable would be swept out from under the
        // variable.
        WeakMapVal(p) -> gc.mark(h, ptr_cast(p))
        WeakRefVal(p) -> gc.mark(h, ptr_cast(p))
        _ -> ()
end mark_value

// The collected object a value points at, or null for one that points at nothing.
//
// **This is what a weak slot asks about**, `gc.is_live` taking the pointer marking decided about --
// and a value with none is one the collector cannot free, so a weak slot holding it never clears.
// An external is the case that matters: it is an interned path rather than an object, so a weak map
// keyed by one is an ordinary identity-keyed map whose entries live as long as the map does.
private object_of(v: Value) -> *u8 = v match
    Str(p) -> ptr_cast(p)
    Array(p) -> ptr_cast(p)
    Bytes(p) -> ptr_cast(p)
    Object(p) -> ptr_cast(p)
    SetVal(p) -> ptr_cast(p)
    MapVal(p) -> ptr_cast(p)
    WeakMapVal(p) -> ptr_cast(p)
    WeakRefVal(p) -> ptr_cast(p)
    Fn(p) -> ptr_cast(p)
    Promise(p) -> ptr_cast(p)
    _ -> null

// Whether marking has decided this value survives. **A value with no object of its own always
// does**, there being nothing for the collector to take away.
private value_survives(h: *gc.Heap, v: Value) -> bool
    val p = object_of(v)

    p == null || gc.is_live(h, p)
end value_survives

// Grey every value in a map. Used by both the object kind and the scope kind.
private mark_map(h: *gc.Heap, m: Map[string, Value])
    var walk = m.walk()

    loop
        walk.next() match
            None -> break
            Some(pair) -> mark_value(h, pair.1)
end mark_map

// -- what each kind holds outside the heap --------------------------------------------------------
//
// **One function per kind, read by three callers that must agree**: the constructor charging what it
// was handed, the mutators charging what a regrow took, and the mark phase recounting what survived.
// A fourth would be a second opinion about the same bytes.

// A string is its bytes. The `string` box itself is a word or two and is not worth counting.
str_payload(o: *StrObj) -> usize = o.s.bytes.len

array_payload(o: *ArrayObj) -> usize = o.items.cap() * sizeof(Value)

bytes_payload(o: *BytesObj) -> usize = o.data.cap()

// Both halves of the compact dict: the entries and the index that probes into them.
object_payload(o: *ObjectObj) -> usize = o.entries.cap() * sizeof(Entry) + o.index.cap() * sizeof(usize)

closure_payload(o: *ClosureObj) -> usize = o.params.cap() * sizeof(string)

promise_payload(o: *PromiseObj) -> usize = o.message.bytes.len + o.waiters.cap() * sizeof(usize)

// A map's table costs a key, a value and the slot's own state per slot. **`Slot` is
// `sysl.container`'s own type and cannot be named from here**, so the width is spelled out -- and
// because the charge and the recount both read this one function, the two agree whatever the real
// figure turns out to be.
private map_slot(key: usize, value: usize) -> usize = key + value + 8

env_payload(o: *EnvObj) -> usize = o.names.capacity() * map_slot(sizeof(string), sizeof(Value)) + o.mutable.capacity() * map_slot(sizeof(string), sizeof(bool))

// Count an object the mark phase has just reached. **Every kind's tracer calls this exactly once per
// collection**, because `gc.drain` traces an object only on the pass that marks it -- which is what
// makes the total an exact recount of the live payload rather than an accumulation.
private mark_payload(n: usize)
    current().payload_marked += n

// **A FINALIZER DOES NOT CREDIT, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.** A finalizer touches no VM state, which is
// what makes `dispose_vm(v)` safe to call for a VM that is not the current one -- a credit there
// would take the bytes off whichever VM happened to be running. The recount in the mark phase is
// what makes the figure right after a sweep, and it needs nothing from the sweep to do it.

// A string owns its sysl `string` and points at nothing, so it needs a finalizer and no tracer.
trace_str(p: *u8, h: *gc.Heap)
    val o: *StrObj = ptr_cast(p)

    mark_payload(str_payload(o))

finalize_str(p: *u8)
    val o: *StrObj = ptr_cast(p)

    // Writing an empty string here releases the one that was there, which is the whole of what a
    // finalizer owes: give back what the object held outside the collected heap.
    o.s = ""

trace_array(p: *u8, h: *gc.Heap)
    val o: *ArrayObj = ptr_cast(p)

    mark_payload(array_payload(o))

    for i in 0..<o.items.len()
        mark_value(h, o.items.at(i))

finalize_array(p: *u8)
    val o: *ArrayObj = ptr_cast(p)

    o.items = buf()

// **A byte buffer holds no `Value`, so the collector walks none of it** -- which is `trace_str`'s
// arrangement and is what makes a megabyte of bytes cost the tracer one object rather than a million
// edges. The `Buf` is counted storage outside the heap, so the finalizer is what gives it back.
trace_bytes(p: *u8, h: *gc.Heap)
    val o: *BytesObj = ptr_cast(p)

    mark_payload(bytes_payload(o))

finalize_bytes(p: *u8)
    val o: *BytesObj = ptr_cast(p)

    o.data = buf()

// **A key is traced as well as a value**, which is new: a field name used to be a sysl `string` that
// the collector never saw, and it is now a `Value` that may be an array or an object of its own.
trace_object(p: *u8, h: *gc.Heap)
    val o: *ObjectObj = ptr_cast(p)

    mark_payload(object_payload(o))

    for i in 0..<o.entries.len()
        val e = o.entries.at(i)

        if !e.dead
            mark_value(h, e.key)
            mark_value(h, e.value)

finalize_object(p: *u8)
    val o: *ObjectObj = ptr_cast(p)

    o.entries = buf()
    o.index = buf()

trace_closure(p: *u8, h: *gc.Heap)
    val o: *ClosureObj = ptr_cast(p)

    mark_payload(closure_payload(o))
    gc.mark(h, ptr_cast(o.scope))

finalize_closure(p: *u8)
    val o: *ClosureObj = ptr_cast(p)

    o.params = buf()

// **A parked machine's own roots are not reached from here.** The waiters are ids, and `mark_vm`
// walks `parked` on its own -- so what this owes is the settled value and nothing else.
trace_promise(p: *u8, h: *gc.Heap)
    val o: *PromiseObj = ptr_cast(p)

    mark_payload(promise_payload(o))
    mark_value(h, o.value)

finalize_promise(p: *u8)
    val o: *PromiseObj = ptr_cast(p)

    o.message = ""
    o.waiters = buf()

trace_env(p: *u8, h: *gc.Heap)
    val o: *EnvObj = ptr_cast(p)

    mark_payload(env_payload(o))
    gc.mark(h, ptr_cast(o.parent))
    mark_map(h, o.names)

finalize_env(p: *u8)
    val o: *EnvObj = ptr_cast(p)

    o.names = map()
    o.mutable = map()

// **A weak map's tracer marks NEITHER HALF, and that is the whole of what makes it weak.** A key
// nothing else holds is free to go; a value is marked by the ephemeron below, and only because its
// key turned out live. The table's `Buf`s are reference counted and belong to a finalizer, which is
// `finalize_object` -- the storage is an `ObjectObj` and only the hooks differ.
// **The storage is still charged even though neither half is marked** -- the table is an `ObjectObj`
// and its two `Buf`s cost what any object's do.
trace_weak_map(p: *u8, h: *gc.Heap)
    val o: *ObjectObj = ptr_cast(p)

    mark_payload(object_payload(o))

// Mark the values whose keys turned out live, and answer whether that marked anything new.
//
// **The answer is what terminates the fixpoint**, so it must be false once there is nothing left to
// do: a value already marked is not marked again, and a value with no object of its own -- a number,
// a string of a kind the collector does not own -- counts as marked, having nothing to lose.
//
// **A value may be some other entry's key**, which is why `collect` runs this again after the
// worklist drains rather than once: `wm.set(a, b)` and `wm.set(b, c)` with only `a` held from
// outside keeps all three, and it takes two passes to find that out.
ephemeron_weak_map(p: *u8, h: *gc.Heap) -> bool
    val o: *ObjectObj = ptr_cast(p)
    var changed = false

    for i in 0..<o.entries.len()
        val e = o.entries.at(i)

        if !e.dead && value_survives(h, e.key) && !value_survives(h, e.value)
            mark_value(h, e.value)
            changed = true

    changed
end ephemeron_weak_map

// Drop the entries whose keys did not survive, before anything is freed.
//
// **The entry is dropped by POSITION and its key is never hashed again**, which is not an
// optimization: hashing a slate value may run a class's own `hash`, and running a program's code
// from inside a collection is the one thing a hook may not do. The hash is on the entry, which is
// what `obj_drop_at` needs to find its slot in the index.
weaken_weak_map(p: *u8, h: *gc.Heap)
    val o: *ObjectObj = ptr_cast(p)

    for i in 0..<o.entries.len()
        val e = o.entries.at(i)

        if !e.dead && !value_survives(h, e.key) then obj_drop_at(o, i)
end weaken_weak_map

// A weak reference points at nothing as far as marking is concerned, which is the definition.
trace_weak_ref(p: *u8, h: *gc.Heap)
    ()

// Null the slot once marking has decided the target is dead.
//
// **This runs before anything is freed**, so the slot is never read after its target's storage has
// gone -- and `Null` is what `deref` then answers, slate having no `undefined` to store.
weaken_weak_ref(p: *u8, h: *gc.Heap)
    var o: *WeakRefObj = ptr_cast(p)

    if !value_survives(h, o.target) then o.target = Null

// A `Kind` must outlive every object naming it and its address must not move, so these are module
// storage. Module storage states its type, which is why each is written out.
//
// process-wide: nine tables of function addresses, built at startup and never written again. A VM
// of its own would hold the same nine, so each is one table and not one per VM.
var str_kind: gc.Kind = gc.Kind(&trace_str, &finalize_str, null, null)
// process-wide: see `str_kind`.
var array_kind: gc.Kind = gc.Kind(&trace_array, &finalize_array, null, null)
// process-wide: see `str_kind`.
var bytes_kind: gc.Kind = gc.Kind(&trace_bytes, &finalize_bytes, null, null)
// process-wide: see `str_kind`.
var object_kind: gc.Kind = gc.Kind(&trace_object, &finalize_object, null, null)
// process-wide: see `str_kind`.
var closure_kind: gc.Kind = gc.Kind(&trace_closure, &finalize_closure, null, null)
// process-wide: see `str_kind`.
var env_kind: gc.Kind = gc.Kind(&trace_env, &finalize_env, null, null)
// process-wide: see `str_kind`.
var promise_kind: gc.Kind = gc.Kind(&trace_promise, &finalize_promise, null, null)

// **The two kinds that use the hooks the other six leave null**, and they are the reason the
// collector has four rather than two. A weak map shares the object's storage and its finalizer and
// differs in nothing else; a weak reference is one slot, so it needs no finalizer at all.
// process-wide: see `str_kind`.
var weak_map_kind: gc.Kind = gc.Kind(&trace_weak_map, &finalize_object, &weaken_weak_map, &ephemeron_weak_map)
// process-wide: see `str_kind`.
var weak_ref_kind: gc.Kind = gc.Kind(&trace_weak_ref, null, &weaken_weak_ref, null)

// -- the roots ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// **`alloc` never collects, so a value held only in a sysl local is invisible to the mark phase.**
// A tree-walker has those everywhere -- the subject of a `match`, a half-built array literal, the
// evaluated arguments of a call -- and none of them is in any scope. So the interpreter pushes what
// it is holding onto these, and pops on the way out.
//
// This is the shadow stack every precise collector's client has to keep, and it is the real cost of
// tracing over refcounting. The alternative -- scanning the machine stack -- is what makes a
// conservative collector a porting project rather than a dependency.
// The two are `current().root_values` and `current().root_envs` -- fields of the VM, see
// `vm_state.sysl`.

// Where the stacks stand now, to be given back to `release` on the way out.
root_mark() -> usize = current().root_values.len()

env_mark() -> usize = current().root_envs.len()

// Hold a value across a point that may collect.
hold(v: Value)
    current().root_values.push(v)

// Let go of everything pushed since a mark.
release(mark: usize)
    current().root_values.truncate(mark)

// Hold a scope across a point that may collect.
hold_env(e: *EnvObj)
    current().root_envs.push(e)

release_env(mark: usize)
    current().root_envs.truncate(mark)

// Everything the program can still reach.
//
// **A top-level function, because a hook is reached by address** and a nested one has no address to
// take. **It reaches the VM through `current()`**, which is what lets it be one function over any
// number of heaps: `gc.collect` hands it the heap it is collecting, and that is `current().heap`
// because the caller asked for it through `slate_heap()`.
roots(h: *gc.Heap)
    // The machine's operand and frame stacks. **These are what the shadow stack below exists to
    // stand in for**, and once the walker is gone most of it goes with it.
    mark_vm(h)

    // What every module exported, which is what a name imported from one is reached through.
    mark_modules(h)

    // Everything the event loop may still call. Nothing else knows these are reachable.
    mark_pending(h)

    // And what a signal may call, which is a table of its own: a watcher is armed rather than
    // outstanding, so it is not one of the things above.
    mark_watches(h)

    // And what a child process and a channel may call, for the reason a watcher's table is separate:
    // a child is neither waiting for an answer nor armed -- it is a process, running.
    mark_children(h)

    // And what a page can still call, which is a table of its own for a watcher's reason: a function
    // bound into a window is neither waiting for an answer nor outstanding on the loop -- it lasts as
    // long as the window does.
#if feature_webview
    mark_windows(h)
#endif

    // And what an actor is holding: the instance of its own declaration, and the promises its
    // handlers answered with. A table of its own for a watcher's reason -- a handler's promise is
    // neither outstanding on the loop nor awaited by anything on this machine.
    mark_actors(h)

    // And the scope every declared type is holding, which is a table of its own for the reason a
    // watcher's is: a shape is not waiting for anything and is not armed either -- it simply lasts
    // as long as the program.
    mark_shapes(h)

    // The answers waiting to be handed back to a suspended call, and the promises that failed with
    // nobody watching. Both are held in module storage and nowhere else.
    mark_scheduler(h)

    for i in 0..<current().root_values.len()
        mark_value(h, current().root_values.at(i))

    for i in 0..<current().root_envs.len()
        gc.mark(h, ptr_cast(current().root_envs.at(i)))

    // The emergency objects a full heap is answered with. Nothing else refers to them until the
    // moment they are handed out, so nothing else would keep them.
    mark_spares(h)
end roots

// How much of the heap is spoken for.
heap_used() -> usize = gc.used(slate_heap())

// How many collections this program has run is `current().collections`, which is what makes a claim
// about *when* slate collects something a test can check rather than something a comment asserts.

// Collect, at a point the interpreter has chosen because it knows what is live.
//
// **The payload count is RECOMPUTED here and not adjusted**, which is what bounds the damage a
// missed charge can do: the tracers add up what every surviving object holds, and that total
// replaces whatever the running estimate had become. So a charge whose credit was forgotten costs a
// schedule that collects a little early until the next collection, and never a figure that grows
// without bound into a false out-of-memory.
collect_now()
    val v = current()
    val began = profile_began()

    v.collections += 1
    v.payload_marked = 0

    gc.collect(slate_heap(), &roots)

    v.payload = v.payload_marked
    profile_collected(began)

// The smallest live set worth collecting over. Below this a program is cheap enough that tracing it
// costs more than the storage it would give back.
// Not `private`: `new_vm` in `vm_state.sysl` starts a VM's `collect_above` here.
const CollectFloor: usize = 262144

// The same question asked about payload, and the answer is four times larger because a byte of
// payload is nothing like a byte of cells to reclaim.
//
// **A collection costs what the OBJECT GRAPH costs and not what the bytes cost.** `gc.collect` walks
// the heap's object list once per phase and the mark phase follows every pointer a live object has,
// so a sweep that hands back a megabyte of string storage costs exactly what one handing back a
// kilobyte of cells costs -- the megabyte is one `finalize` call. **So the two figures cannot share
// a floor.** 256 KiB is a sensible smallest-worth-collecting for cells, where it is thousands of
// objects; for payload it is one medium string, which is why a program building text crossed its
// threshold every turn.
//
// **THE FLOOR IS WHAT A PROGRAM MAY HOLD IN DROPPED PAYLOAD, AND IT BUYS SPEED WITH RESIDENT
// MEMORY -- 4 MiB WAS MEASURED AND REFUSED.** `bench/strings` churns 22 GB of string copies over a
// live set of 383 KB, and the floor is what decides how much of that stands at once: at 1 MiB it
// runs 45,627 collections in 823 ms and peaks at 30.9 MB resident, at 4 MiB 10,964 collections in
// 761 ms and **83.1 MB** -- against 123,925 collections, 908 ms and 14.3 MB with one shared
// threshold. Resident memory grows by far more than the floor itself, the freed strings being every
// size the program grew through, so the larger floor buys 8% of that benchmark for six times the
// memory. A server holding a heap all day is what this language is for, so the smaller number wins.
// Not `private`: `new_vm` in `vm_state.sysl` starts a VM's `payload_above` here.
const PayloadFloor: usize = 1048576

// How much the live set may grow to before the next collection is `current().collect_above`. Raised
// after every collection to a multiple of what survived it.

// How much bigger than the surviving set the heap may get before collecting again.
//
// **Two means half the work a program does is collecting a heap that has doubled**, which is the
// usual place to start and is what most generational-free collectors settle on.
private const Headroom: usize = 2

// Collect if enough has been allocated to be worth it.
//
// **Called only at a statement boundary, and that is the whole of the safety argument.** `alloc`
// never collects, so the one moment a collection can happen is one the interpreter chose -- and it
// chose the top of a statement, where what is live is the scope chain plus whatever the frames above
// have pushed onto the shadow stack. A collection anywhere else would sweep a value some sysl local
// was halfway through building.
//
// **The number read is `live_size` and the threshold moves, and both halves were wrong here.** This
// asked whether `used` had passed a *fixed* 256 KiB, and `used` is the bump high-water mark, which
// never falls -- so once a program had ever allocated that much it ran a full mark-sweep at **every
// statement** for the rest of its life, reclaiming almost nothing each time. A 20,000-iteration loop
// building one small object per turn ran **19,629 collections**. Nothing was wrong with the answers,
// which is why it went unnoticed: the only symptom was speed.
//
// Reading `live_size` fixes half of it. The other half is that a fixed threshold puts a program
// whose genuine live set sits above it straight back into the same loop -- so the threshold is
// raised to a multiple of what survived, which is what makes the cost of collecting proportional to
// the garbage produced rather than to the statements executed.
// **The number read is cells PLUS PAYLOAD, and reading cells alone was the third half of the same
// mistake.** A slate string is a cell of a few words owning a sysl `string`; an array owns a `Buf`,
// an object a `Map`, a byte buffer its bytes -- none of it inside the block the collector manages.
// A program making and dropping four thousand one-megabyte buffers therefore moved `live_size` by
// almost nothing, no collection was ever due, and 1.67 GB of garbage piled up that a collection
// would have taken. **Payload is therefore SCHEDULED**, and `Headroom ×` applies to it for the
// reason it applies to the cells: a threshold below a program's genuine live payload puts it
// straight back into collect-every-statement.
//
// **The ceiling is read here too, and it is the same combined figure.** A collection is attempted
// first whichever of the two questions brought us here, so a program is never told it has run out of
// memory over storage a sweep was about to give back.
//
// **CELLS AND PAYLOAD ARE TWO SCHEDULES, AND ONE THRESHOLD OVER THE COMBINED FIGURE WAS THE FOURTH
// HALF OF THE SAME MISTAKE.** Doubling is right for both, and the FLOOR is not: a single threshold
// of `max(Headroom × (cells + payload), CollectFloor)` gives payload a smallest-worth-collecting of
// 256 KiB, which one medium string is already past. A program whose live set is one growing string
// then allocates about as much payload per turn as it is holding, crosses its own threshold every
// turn, and collects almost once a statement -- 123,884 collections over 150,000 turns of
// `bench/strings`, a quarter of its wall time, each one walking the object graph to hand back the
// previous copy of the string. So each figure is compared against a threshold of its own, raised to
// `Headroom ×` what survived with its own floor under it, and a collection either question asks for
// re-schedules both.
maybe_collect()
    val v = current()
    val capped = v.payload_capped
    val cells = gc.live_size(slate_heap())
    val in_use = cells + v.payload

    // **The high-water mark is taken HERE and not in `charge`**, which is what makes it the figure
    // the schedule is answerable for: the largest combined size the schedule ever let stand at a
    // statement boundary, read where the decision to collect is made and before it is made.
    if in_use > v.high_water then v.high_water = in_use

    if (capped && in_use > v.heap_limit) || cells > v.collect_above || v.payload > v.payload_above
        collect_now()

        val cells_left = gc.live_size(slate_heap()) * Headroom
        val payload_left = v.payload * Headroom

        v.collect_above = if cells_left > CollectFloor then cells_left else CollectFloor
        v.payload_above = if payload_left > PayloadFloor then payload_left else PayloadFloor

    if capped && heap_in_use() > v.heap_limit then v.heap_exhausted = true
end maybe_collect

// Give the heap back, and run every finalizer still outstanding.
//
// **Without it a finalizer never runs for anything still reachable when the program stops**, so
// every sysl string and `Buf` a live object holds would leak. It is also what lets two programs run
// in one process, which every test in this repo does.
reset_heap()
    gc.dispose(slate_heap())

    // **Rebuilt rather than reused, because the limit may have moved.** `dispose` hands every block
    // back to the free list, which is the right thing for a heap of the same size and the wrong one
    // for a heap that has just changed size -- the free list would still describe the old extent.
    val v = current()

    v.heap = gc.heap(v.region, v.heap_limit)
    v.root_values.clear()
    v.root_envs.clear()
    v.collections = 0
    v.collect_above = CollectFloor
    v.payload_above = PayloadFloor

    // `dispose` ran every finalizer, so nothing is held outside the heap any more.
    v.payload = 0
    v.payload_marked = 0
    v.high_water = 0

    v.heap_exhausted = false
    reserve_spares()

// -- allocation ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// **A full heap answers null, and writing through that is a wild write.** The heap does not grow, so
// a program that outruns it has to be told rather than crashed: each of these collects first if the
// heap is nearly full, and `out_of_memory` reports the one case where that did not help.
//
// The collection here is safe for the same reason the one in `exec_block` is: these are only reached
// while the interpreter is between sub-expressions, with everything live either in a scope or on the
// shadow stack.

// Whether the last allocation failed is `current().heap_exhausted`. Read at the statement boundary,
// where there is a span to point at -- an allocator has no idea which line of the program it is
// serving.

// One object of every kind, taken while the heap is empty and never given back.
//
// **These are what a full heap is answered with, and without them it was answered with `null`.** The
// comment above has always said that writing through a full heap's answer is a wild write, and every
// one of the constructors below did exactly that: `alloc_or_collect` set `heap_exhausted` and
// returned null, and `new_str` wrote `o.s = s` straight through it. The flag is read at the *next*
// statement boundary, which the process never reached -- a slate program that outgrew the heap died
// with a bare SIGSEGV, no diagnostic, and none of the output it had already produced.
//
// A reserve is a real object in the heap with a real header, so it can be written to, traced and
// marked like any other. Handing the same one out twice makes two slate values share it, which is
// wrong and does not matter: the program stops at the end of the statement that got it.
// They are the nine `current().spare_*` fields -- see `vm_state.sysl`.

// Take the reserves. Called with an empty heap, where the allocation cannot fail.
private reserve_spares()
    val v = current()

    v.spare_str = ptr_cast(gc.alloc(slate_heap(), sizeof(StrObj), &str_kind))
    v.spare_array = ptr_cast(gc.alloc(slate_heap(), sizeof(ArrayObj), &array_kind))
    v.spare_bytes = ptr_cast(gc.alloc(slate_heap(), sizeof(BytesObj), &bytes_kind))
    v.spare_object = ptr_cast(gc.alloc(slate_heap(), sizeof(ObjectObj), &object_kind))
    v.spare_closure = ptr_cast(gc.alloc(slate_heap(), sizeof(ClosureObj), &closure_kind))
    v.spare_env = ptr_cast(gc.alloc(slate_heap(), sizeof(EnvObj), &env_kind))
    v.spare_promise = ptr_cast(gc.alloc(slate_heap(), sizeof(PromiseObj), &promise_kind))

    // **The weak pair gets a reserve of its own rather than borrowing the object's**, because the
    // kind is written into the header at the allocation: a reserve taken under `object_kind` handed
    // out as a weak map would be traced strongly and weakened by nothing.
    v.spare_weak_map = ptr_cast(gc.alloc(slate_heap(), sizeof(ObjectObj), &weak_map_kind))
    v.spare_weak_ref = ptr_cast(gc.alloc(slate_heap(), sizeof(WeakRefObj), &weak_ref_kind))

    // A zeroed payload is a valid object of each of these kinds except the ones holding a `Buf` or a
    // `Map`, which a tracer walks -- and a zero `Buf` is an empty one, so all nine are safe to trace
    // from the moment they exist.
    v.heap_exhausted = false
end reserve_spares

// Keep the reserves alive. They are rooted rather than special-cased in the sweep, because a rooted
// object is one the collector already knows how to keep.
private mark_spares(h: *gc.Heap)
    val v = current()

    gc.mark(h, ptr_cast(v.spare_str))
    gc.mark(h, ptr_cast(v.spare_array))
    gc.mark(h, ptr_cast(v.spare_bytes))
    gc.mark(h, ptr_cast(v.spare_object))
    gc.mark(h, ptr_cast(v.spare_closure))
    gc.mark(h, ptr_cast(v.spare_env))
    gc.mark(h, ptr_cast(v.spare_promise))
    gc.mark(h, ptr_cast(v.spare_weak_map))
    gc.mark(h, ptr_cast(v.spare_weak_ref))

// Try once, collect, try again -- and answer the reserve rather than null when even that fails.
//
// **The caller is going to write through whatever comes back**, so this may not answer null. What it
// does instead is say so in `heap_exhausted`, which the statement boundary reports against a line.
private alloc_or_collect(size: usize, kind: *gc.Kind, spare: *u8) -> *u8
    var p = gc.alloc(slate_heap(), size, kind)

    if p != null then return p

    collect_now()

    p = gc.alloc(slate_heap(), size, kind)

    if p != null then return p

    current().heap_exhausted = true
    spare
end alloc_or_collect
// **Each of these charges what it was handed**, the object becoming the owner of storage that was a
// caller's local a moment ago. The mutators below charge what a regrow took; between them that is
// every byte a slate value holds outside the heap.
// **THE COUNT IS A PARAMETER SO THAT NO CALLER CAN FORGET IT**, which is why there is no
// `new_str(s)` to reach for: a string's length is O(1) only if every cell that is ever made carries
// the number, and a constructor that worked it out itself would scan text whose count the caller
// already knew. Nearly every string a program makes is derived from one it already had --
// concatenation adds two counts, a slice subtracts two positions, `repeat` multiplies -- and the
// count travels with it for nothing.
//
// `chars` must be the number of characters `s` holds. It is the caller's claim and nothing checks
// it, exactly as `new_array`'s `Buf` is; `new_str_scanned` is the way to say "I do not know".
//
// **The cursor starts at the front**, which is where a zeroed cell already is.
new_str_counted(s: string, chars: usize) -> *StrObj
    var o: *StrObj = ptr_cast(alloc_or_collect(sizeof(StrObj), &str_kind, ptr_cast(current().spare_str)))

    o.s = s
    o.chars = chars
    o.cursor_char = 0
    o.cursor_byte = 0
    charge(str_payload(o))
    o

// A string whose count nobody knows, which is one walk of the bytes.
//
// **This is for text arriving from OUTSIDE the machine** -- a file, a socket, a child's output,
// decoded bytes, a database column -- where nothing upstream counted anything. Everywhere else the
// count is already in hand and `new_str_counted` is what says so.
new_str_scanned(s: string) -> *StrObj = new_str_counted(s, char_count(s))

new_array(items: Buf[Value]) -> *ArrayObj
    var o: *ArrayObj = ptr_cast(alloc_or_collect(sizeof(ArrayObj), &array_kind, ptr_cast(current().spare_array)))

    o.items = items
    charge(array_payload(o))
    o

// **The `Buf` is taken by value and this object becomes its owner**, which is `new_str`'s bargain: the
// caller's copy dies with the caller's frame and the finalizer is what takes the count to zero.
new_bytes(data: Buf[u8]) -> *BytesObj
    var o: *BytesObj = ptr_cast(alloc_or_collect(sizeof(BytesObj), &bytes_kind, ptr_cast(current().spare_bytes)))

    o.data = data
    charge(bytes_payload(o))
    o

new_object() -> *ObjectObj
    var o: *ObjectObj = ptr_cast(alloc_or_collect(sizeof(ObjectObj), &object_kind, ptr_cast(current().spare_object)))

    o.entries = buf()

    // **A new table has no index and allocates none**, which `table.sysl`'s `SmallTable` is the
    // whole of: a lookup walks the entries until the table outgrows the small case, and an object
    // with two fields never does. An empty index is what says a table is in that case.
    o.index = buf()
    o.live = 0
    o.frozen = false
    o.by_identity = false
    charge(object_payload(o))
    o

// **A weak map is `new_object`'s twin under another kind**, and the two must be built alike --
// including the index it does not have.
new_weak_map() -> *ObjectObj
    var o: *ObjectObj = ptr_cast(alloc_or_collect(sizeof(ObjectObj), &weak_map_kind, ptr_cast(current().spare_weak_map)))

    o.entries = buf()
    o.index = buf()
    o.live = 0
    o.frozen = false
    o.by_identity = true
    charge(object_payload(o))
    o

new_weak_ref(target: Value) -> *WeakRefObj
    var o: *WeakRefObj = ptr_cast(alloc_or_collect(sizeof(WeakRefObj), &weak_ref_kind, ptr_cast(current().spare_weak_ref)))

    o.target = target
    o

new_closure(params: Buf[string], body: usize, scope: *EnvObj) -> *ClosureObj
    var o: *ClosureObj = ptr_cast(alloc_or_collect(sizeof(ClosureObj), &closure_kind, ptr_cast(current().spare_closure)))

    o.params = params
    o.body = body
    o.scope = scope
    charge(closure_payload(o))
    o

new_promise() -> *PromiseObj
    var o: *PromiseObj = ptr_cast(alloc_or_collect(sizeof(PromiseObj), &promise_kind, ptr_cast(current().spare_promise)))

    o.value = Null
    o.settled = false
    o.failed = false
    o.message = ""
    o.at = point(0)
    o.src = 0
    o.quit = false
    o.quit_status = 0
    o.waiters = buf()
    o.handled = false
    o.orphaned = false
    o

new_env(parent: *EnvObj) -> *EnvObj
    var o: *EnvObj = ptr_cast(alloc_or_collect(sizeof(EnvObj), &env_kind, ptr_cast(current().spare_env)))

    o.names = map()
    o.mutable = map()
    o.parent = parent
    o

// -- growing a payload ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// **A container that GROWS is where the charge would otherwise be missed**, and missing it is the
// same hole the constructors close wearing another hat: an array built by pushing a million elements
// is one small cell whose `Buf` has quietly taken sixteen megabytes. Each of these measures the
// capacity either side of the operation and charges the difference, which is nothing at all on the
// pushes that fit in storage the buffer already had.
//
// **`pop`, `remove`, `truncate` and `clear` are NOT here and need nothing**, a `Buf` keeping its
// storage when its count falls -- so a program that empties a buffer is still holding what it grew,
// and saying otherwise would report pages that are still committed as free.

// Append to a slate array.
array_push(o: *ArrayObj, v: Value)
    val was = o.items.cap()

    o.items.push(v)
    charge((o.items.cap() - was) * sizeof(Value))

// Put a value at a position, moving everything from there on up one.
array_insert(o: *ArrayObj, i: usize, v: Value)
    val was = o.items.cap()

    o.items.insert(i, v)
    charge((o.items.cap() - was) * sizeof(Value))

// Append one byte to a byte buffer.
bytes_push(o: *BytesObj, b: u8)
    val was = o.data.cap()

    o.data.push(b)
    charge(o.data.cap() - was)

// Append a run of bytes to a byte buffer.
bytes_extend(o: *BytesObj, bs: []const u8)
    val was = o.data.cap()

    o.data.extend(bs)
    charge(o.data.cap() - was)

// Hand a byte buffer's storage back, which is what `transfer` does to the sender's.
//
// **`clear` would not do it**: a `Buf` that is emptied keeps its storage, so a transferred buffer
// would read as empty to the program while still holding every page it grew.
bytes_release(o: *BytesObj)
    credit(bytes_payload(o))
    o.data = buf()

// Append an entry to an object's table.
entry_push(o: *ObjectObj, e: Entry)
    val was = o.entries.cap()

    o.entries.push(e)
    charge((o.entries.cap() - was) * sizeof(Entry))

// Note that an object's two buffers have been rebuilt, which is what a rehash does to them.
object_recharged(o: *ObjectObj, was: usize)
    recharge(was, object_payload(o))

// Add a parked machine to what a promise will hand its answer to.
waiter_push(o: *PromiseObj, id: usize)
    val was = o.waiters.cap()

    o.waiters.push(id)
    charge((o.waiters.cap() - was) * sizeof(usize))

// Give a promise the complaint it failed with.
promise_says(o: *PromiseObj, message: string)
    credit(o.message.bytes.len)
    o.message = message
    charge(o.message.bytes.len)

// Note that a scope's tables have grown, which is what declaring a name into one may do.
env_recharged(o: *EnvObj, was: usize)
    recharge(was, env_payload(o))
